        <div id="reel-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Wall Mount, Cart, or Retractable
          </h2>

          <p>
            The three formats suit genuinely different properties, and choosing the wrong one is the most common reason a reel ends up unused with the hose back in a pile on the ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wall Mount Reels</h3>

          <p>
            Fixed to a wall near the spigot, connected by a short leader hose. The most compact option, the most stable, and generally the most durable since there are no wheels or spring mechanisms. The limitation is that the hose always originates from one point, so a property with widely separated watering areas may need two reels or a very long hose. Requires mounting into studs or masonry, since a full hose is heavy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hose Carts</h3>

          <p>
            Wheeled reels you pull to wherever you are working. The right answer when your watering areas are scattered, when you cannot or do not want to drill into the house, or when you want to store the reel indoors between seasons. The trade-off is wheels: small plastic wheels struggle badly on grass and gravel, so pneumatic tires are worth paying for on anything but paved routes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Retractable Reels</h3>

          <p>
            Spring-loaded reels that wind the hose back automatically after a tug. By far the most convenient, which matters because convenience determines whether a reel actually gets used. Usually enclosed, which gives the best UV protection. The costs are mechanical complexity, weight requiring solid mounting, and a more involved job if you ever need to replace the hose.
          </p>

