        <!-- SECTION 4: What Actually Fails on a Hose Reel -->
        <div id="what-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Actually Fails on a Hose Reel
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hose reels fail in a small number of predictable ways, and knowing them tells you exactly which specifications are worth paying for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Swivel Joint</h3>

          <p>
            Water has to travel from a fixed inlet into a rotating drum, and that transition happens at a swivel with seals inside. It is the highest-wear component on any reel and the most common source of leaks. Brass swivels with replaceable seals can be serviced; sealed plastic assemblies cannot, which means a leak there ends the reel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Crank Gear</h3>

          <p>
            Winding 100 feet of water-filled hose puts real torque through the crank mechanism. Plastic gears strip under that load, particularly if the hose has bunched at one end of the drum and binds. Metal gearing, or a direct-drive design with no gear reduction at all, avoids the failure entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frame Flex</h3>

          <p>
            A full hose reel carries a surprising amount of weight, and a frame that flexes under it concentrates stress at mounting points and welds. Visible flex when cranking is the clearest warning sign that a reel will crack within a few seasons, and it is easy to check in the store.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mounting Failure</h3>

          <p>
            The most dramatic failure, and entirely preventable. A hundred feet of water-filled hose plus the reel can approach sixty pounds, and that load hanging off siding or drywall anchors will eventually pull out and take part of the wall with it. Mount into studs or solid masonry with appropriate fasteners.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Leader Hose Nobody Buys</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A wall-mounted reel needs a short leader hose connecting the spigot to the reel inlet, and it is almost never included. Buy a good one, usually 3 to 6 feet, rather than cutting down an old hose, because this short section takes constant flexing every time the reel pivots and it is the connection most likely to leak. A quality leader hose costs little and prevents a persistent drip.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Capacity, Hose Compatibility, and Sizing -->
        <div id="capacity-and-compatibility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Capacity, Hose Compatibility, and Sizing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reel capacity figures are always quoted for a specific hose diameter, and getting the match wrong leads to a hose that will not fit or a drum that jams.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Capacity ratings assume 5/8-inch hose.</strong> Nearly every reel states its capacity in feet of 5/8-inch hose, which is the residential standard. If you use 3/4-inch hose, actual capacity drops substantially, often by a third or more, because the thicker hose takes up more drum volume per foot. Check the rating for your actual diameter before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy more capacity than your hose length.</strong> A reel loaded to its exact rated capacity winds tightly and binds, making cranking harder and increasing the chance of the hose bunching. Leaving headroom, choosing a 150-foot reel for a 100-foot hose, makes the reel noticeably easier to use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Stiff hoses need bigger drums.</strong> A rubber hose or a cold vinyl hose resists bending around a tight radius, and forcing it onto a small drum both makes cranking harder and creates permanent set in the hose. Larger-diameter drums are easier on every hose and essential for rubber ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expandable hoses do not belong on reels.</strong> Expandable hoses contract to a bunched, irregular shape that will not wind evenly on a drum, and the winding tension damages the latex tube. Store them in a hose pot, a bucket, or on a wide hook instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the inlet thread and swivel type.</strong> Most reels use standard garden hose thread, but the orientation of the inlet matters for how the leader hose routes. A reel whose inlet points into a wall will kink the leader hose permanently, so look at the geometry before mounting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Installation and Freeze Protection -->
        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Installation and Freeze Protection
          </h2>

          <p>
            A reel is only as good as its mounting, and a reel left connected through a freeze can damage both the hose and the plumbing behind the spigot.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Find the studs.</strong> Locate wall studs with a stud finder and mount through them with lag screws long enough to reach solid wood. On brick or block, use appropriate masonry anchors rated well above the expected load. Never rely on siding, drywall anchors, or plastic wall plugs for a reel that will carry a full hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mount at a working height.</strong> Roughly waist height puts the crank at a comfortable position and lets the hose feed off the drum without dragging on the ground. Too low and the hose scrapes; too high and cranking becomes awkward.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Leave clearance for the pivot.</strong> Pivoting reels need room to swing, and mounting one tight into an inside corner defeats the pivot entirely. Check the swing arc before drilling.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Disconnect and drain before freezing weather.</strong> Water left in a hose on a reel freezes and splits the hose, and water left in the leader hose and spigot can freeze back into the wall and burst a pipe. Disconnect the leader hose from the spigot, drain the main hose fully, and if your spigot is not frost-free, shut off and drain the interior supply valve.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the reel turns freely before the first full load.</strong> Assemble the reel, connect it, and wind the empty hose on once before putting it into service. Binding, rubbing, or a drum that will not turn smoothly is far easier to diagnose and fix with an empty drum than with a hundred feet of water-filled hose wrapped around it, and a reel that binds when empty will strip its gears when loaded.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Consider bringing carts inside.</strong> One quiet advantage of a cart over a wall mount is that the whole assembly, hose included, can be wheeled into a garage for the winter, which protects both the hose and the reel mechanism from freeze damage and UV over the off season.
          </p>
        </div>

