            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are retractable hose reels worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if convenience is what determines whether you actually put the hose away. A retractable reel winds itself after a short tug, which removes the friction that leads to hoses left in a pile. The trade-offs are more mechanical complexity, greater weight requiring solid mounting, and a more involved job if you eventually need to replace the hose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I mount a hose reel to a wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Locate studs with a stud finder and mount through them with lag screws long enough to bite solid wood, or use masonry anchors rated well above the load on brick or block. A hundred feet of water-filled hose plus the reel can approach sixty pounds, so siding, drywall anchors, and plastic plugs are not adequate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much hose does a hose reel hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typical residential reels hold between 100 and 300 feet, and the rating almost always assumes 5/8-inch hose. Three-quarter-inch hose takes up considerably more drum volume, often cutting capacity by a third. Buy a reel rated above your actual hose length, since a drum loaded to its exact capacity winds tightly and binds.
              </p>
