        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Hose Reel Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hose reel problems mostly come from mounting, sizing, and one component that nobody thinks about until it leaks.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting Into Siding Instead of Structure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A reel with a full hose can approach sixty pounds, and that load hanging off siding or drywall anchors will eventually pull free, taking part of the wall with it. Locate studs or use proper masonry anchors, and use lag screws long enough to reach solid material. This is the failure that turns a cheap reel into an expensive repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling the Drum to Its Exact Rated Capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A reel loaded to its stated maximum winds tightly, binds against the frame, and makes cranking far harder than it should be, which is also how plastic gears strip. Choose a reel rated well above your actual hose length so there is room for the hose to lie loosely on the drum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Up an Old Hose for a Leader</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The short leader hose between the spigot and a wall-mounted reel flexes every time the reel pivots, and it is the connection most likely to develop a persistent drip. Using a cut-down old hose with a clamped-on fitting almost guarantees that leak. Buy a proper leader hose with factory couplings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Hose on the Reel Through Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water trapped in a hose on a reel freezes and splits the tube, and water left in the leader hose can freeze back into the wall and burst a supply pipe. Disconnect at the spigot, drain the hose completely, and where possible bring cart-mounted reels indoors for the season.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

